WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.029
Whether you're a diehard Game of Thrones fan
or a casual viewer, it's fun to hunt for any

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:10.560
clues you can find from episode to episode.
And the eighth and final season is no exception.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:15.339
Here's a look at many of the Game of Thrones
details you missed in the last six episodes

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.340
of the series.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.700
One of the most unexpected moments of the
eighth season premiere actually came in the

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.430
show's opening credits, which features an
entirely new sequence with plenty of Easter

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:25.920
eggs.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.960
The most notable change came right at the
start, with a reminder that the Wall that

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.340
once protected the Seven Kingdoms from the
threat of the White Walkers has been breached,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.410
thanks to the Night King's brand-new zombie
dragon at the end of last season. The introduction

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.870
also added Last Hearth, an abandoned Northern
castle formerly home to the Umber family,

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.120
who were loyal to the Starks…right up until
they were all slaughtered by wights.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:54.160
The map even shows us the path that the White
Walkers took to the climactic battle of Winterfell,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.850
with the white tiles that made up the snowy
ground flipping to black to represent their

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:04.140
movement. After the climactic battle in "The
Long Night," though, those tiles stopped flipping,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.139
indicating that the White Walkers are actually
finished after all. It's not just the events

00:01:09.139 --> 00:01:13.229
of Season Eight that have left their mark
on Westeros, either. Images displayed on the

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:18.400
bands around the model sun depict past events
like the third season's infamous Red Wedding.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:23.439
There's also an image of three dragons looking
at a comet, which represents the Red Comet,

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:27.810
said to fall when dragons appear, and which
made its own appearance in season two of the

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:28.300
show:

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.260
"That comet means one thing, boy: dragons."

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.999
Finally, in one of the coolest tweaks to the
opening sequence, the final episode shows

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:44.729
the damage that's been done to both Winterfell
and King's Landing. Like the actual city it

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:48.670
represents, the intricate clockworks that
raise the towers of the Red Keep on the map

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:53.580
have been broken, leaving the machinery stuttering
as it tries to raise them one last time.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.531
The last time we saw Tormund Giantsbane and
Beric Dondarrion at the end of Season 7, they

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.120
were caught up in the Night King's first strike,
watching as he brought down the Wall. When

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.520
we finally get to check in with them in Season
8, they're tracking the destruction that the

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:12.720
White Walkers have left in their wake as they
traveled beyond the Wall, and they find a

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.819
pretty disturbing message left by the Night
King. The young Ned Umber, turned into a wight

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:22.819
and pinned to a wall amidst severed limbs,
provides the season's best jump scare when

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.849
he reawakens and has to be set aflame. It's
one of the most distressing images in the

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.319
series' recent memory, with Umber's dead body
at the center of a spiral pattern made up

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.620
of body parts, but it's not the first time
audiences have seen this pattern.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.560
Since the very first episode of the show,
the White Walkers have long been associated

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:46.510
with spirals and circles, from arranging the
body parts of their victims to their sacred

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:51.220
place in the North and the cave drawings that
Jon showed Daenerys in the seventh season.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.360
Perhaps most crucially, the spirals can be
seen in the scene that showed the Children

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.969
of the Forest creating the first Walker, suggesting
that these shapes have more to do with the

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:02.700
lore of the Walkers than viewers fully understand.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.620
When audiences first met Theon Greyjoy, he
was arrogant and dismissive, especially to

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.940
Jon Snow, letting audiences know that perhaps
Theon needed to be taken down a peg.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:14.640
"Right, give it here."

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.940
"I take orders from your father, not you."

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.439
Eight seasons later, and Theon's been brought
about as low as any character possibly could.

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:28.659
He's been psychologically broken from emotional
and physical torture, leaving him a beaten

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:33.569
man struggling to reclaim himself and fight
for a cause he believes in. After several

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:39.219
years of terrible behavior, Theon has fully
redeemed himself by saving Sansa's life and

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.560
proving that he's willing to sacrifice himself
to do the right thing.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.680
Now, in the show's final season, Theon returned
to Winterfell to fight for the Starks, even

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:53.079
though his sister, Yara, set off to retake
the Iron Islands. In doing this, Theon's journey

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:57.939
has come full circle. After starting out with
the Starks before abandoning them, he has

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:02.590
finally come home to stand with them one last
time, completing the arc of one of the show's

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.400
most troubled characters.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.220
"You're a good man."

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.400
Before she used it to put a permanent end
to the army of the dead, Arya's Valyrian steel

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:18.140
dagger already had a pretty notable history.
It was first seen in the show's second episode,

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:23.160
when it was used in an attempt to murder Bran
Stark after his fall from the tower, but it

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.190
really belonged to Littlefinger himself, Lord
Petyr Baelish. From there, it stayed with

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:32.970
Littlefinger until he gave it back to Bran,
who gave it to Arya, who, naturally, used

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.320
it to execute Littlefinger during his season
7 trial.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:41.800
During the episode's big climax, as the Night
King makes his way towards Bran, Arya's training

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:46.660
helps her silently moves through the Godswood
until she gets to the Night King himself,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.380
destroying him with Littlefinger's dagger
to save the brother who gave it to her. This

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.540
knife has had quite the journey where Bran
is concerned, so it seems oddly fitting that

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.840
the weapon once intended to kill him is what
saved not just him, but every human fighting

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:01.840
at Winterfell.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.180
Cersei spent the Battle of Winterfell waiting
out her enemies, so when they finally arrive

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.240
at King's Landing to try to force her surrender,
she was ready to strike.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.620
"Maybe Cersei will win and kill us all. That
would solve our problems."

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:22.410
Before the fight began, though, she had one
last brutal insult to hand down to Daenerys

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:28.720
and her forces. After her assault on Daenerys'
ships near Dragonstone, Cersei captures Missandei,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.090
Daenerys' translator, confidante, and best
friend, and it's immediately clear that Cersei

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:38.260
will use her hostage however she has to in
order to achieve her bottom line.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.560
"If you have any last words, now is the time."

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.590
In a brutal display of power, Cersei orders
the beheading of poor Missandei, who gives

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:53.720
her life willingly for her chosen queen, bravely
standing in support the woman who freed her

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:59.190
from her chains. However, Cersei's decision
to decapitate Missandei wound up having much

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:04.100
larger consequences than she realized. It
was one of the final things that drove Daenerys

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.840
over the edge, leading directly to the destruction
of King's Landing, and to Cersei having a

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.160
couple tons of bricks dropped on her head.
She shouldn't have been surprised, though:

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.850
the last time a blonde Lannister ruler vindictively
cut off someone's head to prove a point, it

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:22.990
was Joffrey ordering Ned Stark's execution,
the act that kicked off seven long years of

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:29.730
warfare and prestige television. In the final
days before her death, Cersei became as sadistic

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.120
as her terrible son.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.780
As one of the only substances that can destroy
a White Walker, Valyrian steel can be tough

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:42.150
to come by. As a result, only a small handful
of characters on Thrones are in possession

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:47.950
of weapons made out of it. One of those characters
was Ned Stark, longtime Warden of the North

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:53.180
and Lord of Winterfell and unfortunately short-lived
Hand of the King to Robert Baratheon, whose

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.920
family sword was a massive piece of Valyrian
steel called Ice. After the honorable but

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.930
naïve Ned figured out that Prince Joffrey
wasn't Robert's biological son, he ended up

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:08.700
losing his head, and Ice was melted down and
remade into two new blades.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.020
The first, christened Widow's Wail by Joffrey,
was barely used before Joffrey met his own

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.860
untimely end. It ended up with Jaime, his
uncle-slash-father.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:23.260
"You said my father was my father, but my
uncle is my father. My father is my uncle!"

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.960
The second sword, Oathkeeper, was gifted to
Brienne of Tarth. Though Jaime and Brienne

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:32.390
spent some time on opposing sides of the conflict
between the Lannisters and the North, they

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:37.790
joined forces again during the Battle of Winterfell.
As they fought side by side, the two parts

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:43.320
of Ned's sword came together once again to
defend House Stark, which feels perfectly

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:43.820
fitting.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.380
"It's reforged from Ned Stark's sword. You
will use it to defend Ned Stark's daughter."

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:55.840
Davos Seaworth might seem prickly at times,
but his soft side always showed around Shireen

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:02.050
Baratheon, daughter of the late Stannis Baratheon.
Shireen was afflicted by greyscale as a baby,

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:06.520
and while healers were able to stop the progression
of the disease, she still bore its scars on

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:11.960
her face, making her somewhat of an outcast.
This bonds her to Davos, especially when he's

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.840
imprisoned by Stannis for turning against
his king. While he's in captivity, Shireen

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:22.110
teaches Davos how to read, and the two end
up forming a real bond. When Davos discovers

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:27.900
later that Shireen has been sacrificed by
Melisandre to help Stannis' cause, he's understandably

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:28.800
devastated.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.510
With that in mind, it's easy to see why Davos
and Gilly, who also learned to read thanks

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:38.260
to Shireen, were particularly struck by a
young, brave girl with a scarred face who

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:43.310
says she wants to fight alongside the soldiers
in Winterfell. To drive the emotional point

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:48.520
home, music associated with Shireen even plays
over the scene, reminding viewers of the young

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.500
princess and her tragic, untimely end.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:57.400
Considering that he held the position of Master
of Whispers, it's no surprise that Lord Varys,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.680
also known as "the Spider," trafficks in secrets.
It's what he's best at, and what he continued

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:07.870
to do right up until the very end of his life.
Having spent time with Daenerys' father, the

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:13.440
Mad King, he was understandably wary of the
Dragon Queen's quick temper and stubborn attitude,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.620
something that he brought up with Tyrion in
a conversation that led to him being executed

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.500
as a traitor.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.660
"Each of us has a choice to make. I pray we
choose wisely."

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:31.440
Varys doesn't plot against Daenerys for long
before he's taken to the beaches of Dragonstone

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.100
and executed by her last living dragon, Drogon,
during the penultimate episode of the series.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:41.580
It shouldn't come as a surprise, however,
that he had a backup plan in place to stop

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:46.750
Dany himself. Before he's hauled off by the
guards, he has a a cryptic conversation with

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:51.860
a servant girl about the fact that Daenerys
isn't eating. In hindsight, it's easy to look

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:56.920
at this as the subtle indication of a last-ditch
effort by Varys to stop Dany before she can

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.300
launch her attack on King's Landing. It's
possible that he was using one of the show's

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:05.960
favorite methods of assassination, trying
to poison Daenerys before she can follow in

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:11.190
her father's footsteps. In the end, her grief
over Missandei and one of her dragons kept

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:15.640
Daenerys safe from eating anything that Varys
may have meddled with, and King's Landing

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:16.720
paid the price.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.200
One of the most shocking moments on the series
came when Gregor Clegane, better known as

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.950
"The Mountain," faced off in trial by combat
against Oberyn Martell. Despite being stabbed

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:32.480
repeatedly, the Mountain ended that fight
by knocking Oberyn down one last time and

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.941
squeezing his head until it exploded. Oberyn's
spear was poisoned, however, and the Mountain

00:10:37.941 --> 00:10:41.560
was given what should've been a fatal dose
of Manticore Venom.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:48.900
"That's not how it ends for you, brother.
You know who's coming for you. You've always

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:49.400
known."

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:55.560
Fortunately for the Mountain, and unfortunately
for almost everyone else, he managed to stick

00:10:55.560 --> 00:11:02.260
around thanks to Cersei's personal Dr. Frankenstein,
Qyburn. He didn't just become a mindless reanimated

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:06.840
corpse, though. If you need any evidence that
there was still at least a little bit of the

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:11.251
Mountain in that massive, horrifying body,
it's that, like all the best fighters, he

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.600
still uses his patented finishing move.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:19.440
When he finally showed up for the long-anticipated
showdown that fans called "CleganeBowl," it

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.390
seemed like the Mountain's little brother
Sandor, nicknamed the Hound, was going to

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:28.390
meet a similar fate. No sword wound could
take down the Mountain, and sure enough, the

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:33.430
Hound got locked into the elder Clegane's
skull-crushing finale. This time, however,

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:38.840
that signature move doesn't guarantee victory.
The Hound overcomes his fear of fire and sends

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:44.060
them both tumbling over the ramparts of the
Red Keep, finally getting an appropriate revenge

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:48.640
for when the Mountain, as a child, shoved
the Hound into a fire and left him scarred

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:49.140
for life.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:54.560
Check out one of our newest videos right here!
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:59.270
stuff are coming soon. Subscribe to our YouTube
channel and hit the bell so you don't miss

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:00.230
a single one.

